.Dd August 31, 2026
.Dt SOWA-PKG 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm sowa-pkg
.Nd the Sowa binary package manager
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar command
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar package ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
installs, upgrades and removes the binary packages Sowa is built from and
published as.
.Pp
A package is an XZ-compressed tar archive carrying the files it owns and three
or four metadata members:
.Pa .PKGINFO ,
what the package is,
.Pa .FILES ,
the manifest of every path with its mode and its SHA-256,
.Pa .MESSAGE ,
a note to show once it is installed, and
.Pa .HOOKS ,
the steps that finish an installation the files alone do not.
.Pp
A repository is a plain HTTPS directory with no server side: an index, a
signature over that index, and the archives.
An update is three GETs and nothing more.
The index is signed with Ed25519 and pins every archive in it by SHA-256, so
that one signature covers the whole repository, and it is the signature rather
than the transport that an update trusts.
An index that does not verify against
.Pa /etc/sowa/keys/sowa-repo.pub
is refused, and a machine with no key refuses every index: it fails closed.
.Pp
A signature says who wrote an index.
It does not say that this is the newest one they wrote, and it does not say the
index is well formed; see
.Sx REPOSITORY FRESHNESS
and
.Sx WHAT IS CHECKED BEFORE ANYTHING IS WRITTEN .
.Pp
Installing never writes through an existing file.
Each archive is unpacked into a staging directory and its files are then moved
into place, so every replacement is a
.Xr rename 2 :
a running program keeps the inode it started with, a shared library is never
truncated under the processes that mapped it, and an interrupted install cannot
leave a half-written binary behind.
This is also why
.Nm
can upgrade itself while it is running.
.Pp
Between unpacking and moving, the staged tree is required to be exactly what the
package's own
.Pa .FILES
says it is: the same paths, types, modes, sizes and contents, with no member the
manifest does not list and nothing that is not a file, a directory or a link.
.Pp
Files below
.Pa /etc
are treated as configuration.
One that has been edited since the package placed it is kept, and the packaged
version is written beside it as
.Pa <file>.tpknew ,
with a line saying so.
.Pp
An upgrade removes the files the old version owned and the new one does not,
with one exception, and it applies anywhere in the filesystem: a file whose
contents are no longer the ones the package installed was written by somebody
after the package placed it, so it is kept, with a line saying which package
has stopped shipping it.
.Ss Steps packages declare
A package may carry hooks: a small, closed set of actions that run around an
installation or a removal.
There is no post-install
.Em script ,
and a package cannot ship one.
Each hook names an event and one of the actions
.Nm
implements itself:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
post-install|setup|/usr/sbin/sowa-guix-setup
post-install|service-enable|guix-daemon
pre-remove|service-stop|sshd
.Ed
.Pp
The events are
.Cm post-install ,
.Cm post-upgrade
and
.Cm pre-remove ;
the actions are
.Cm service-start ,
.Cm service-stop ,
.Cm service-restart ,
.Cm service-enable ,
.Cm service-disable
and
.Cm setup .
The service actions run an init script that is already on the machine.
.Cm setup
runs a program the package itself owns - looked up in that package's own
manifest first - so a hook cannot name
.Pa /bin/sh ,
a path another package placed, or anything that was not in the archive whose
hash the signed index pinned.
Every hook that runs is printed before it runs, and
.Ic sowa-pkg hooks
prints them all without unpacking anything.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "upgrade [package...]"
.It Cm update
Fetch the repository index and its signature, verify the signature, and replace
the local copy.
Says afterwards how many installed packages are out of date and warns when an
installed package is newer than the repository.
.Pp
The index has to be well formed and current as well as signed: see
.Sx WHAT IS CHECKED BEFORE ANYTHING IS WRITTEN
and
.Sx REPOSITORY FRESHNESS .
An index that fails any of that is refused and the one this machine has been
using is left in place.
.It Cm list Op Fl -installed
What is installed and at which version, with the repository's version noted
where it differs.
This is the default.
.It Cm list Fl -upgradable
Only installed packages whose version differs from the repository, labelled as
an upgrade or a downgrade.
.It Cm list Fl -available
What the repository offers and this system does not have, which is the question
the word asks.
.It Cm list Fl -all
Everything, installed and not, in two version columns.
.Ql -
under
.Em installed
is a package this system does not have;
.Ql -
under
.Em repository
is one the index no longer carries.
.It Cm info Ar package ...
What a package is, where it came from, how many paths it owns, the steps it
declares and any note it left.
Reports the installed side, the repository side, or both.
.It Cm files Ar package
Every path the installed package owns, directories excluded.
.It Cm owns Ar path
Which installed package owns a path.
.It Cm install Ar package ...
Install packages and everything they depend on.
Prints the plan and the total download and asks before doing anything.
On an interactive terminal, each download uses a compact meter with its
percentage, transferred size, average speed and estimated time remaining.
.Cm add
is the same command.
.It Cm upgrade Op Ar package ...
Bring the named packages, or the whole system, up to what the repository
offers.
If the repository has an older version, labels the operation as a downgrade and
warns before asking for confirmation.
With no arguments it also says how many packages in the repository are not
installed.
.It Cm remove Ar package ...
Remove packages and the files they own.
Refuses to remove a package another installed package depends on, or one of the
base system's own.
.Cm uninstall
is the same command.
.It Cm hooks Op Ar package ...
Print the steps packages declare, without running any of them.
.It Cm verify Op Ar package ...
Re-check installed files against the SHA-256 the manifest recorded: missing
files, changed symbolic links, and content that no longer matches.
A modified file under
.Pa /etc
is reported as a modified configuration file rather than as a problem, since
that is what configuration is for.
.It Cm clean Op Fl -all
Delete cached archives the index no longer references, or with
.Fl -all
the whole cache.
.It Cm help
Print the usage summary.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--download-only"
.It Fl y , Fl -yes
Do not ask for confirmation.
.It Fl -reinstall
Install again even at the version already installed.
.It Fl -download-only
Fetch the archives into the cache and stop without installing anything.
.It Fl -no-hooks
Do not run the steps packages declare.
On a removal this also means a package whose
.Cm pre-remove
step failed can be removed anyway.
.It Fl -allow-downgrade
Install a package the repository has an older version of than the one on this
machine.
Without it a downgrade is never done: a package named on the command line is
refused with this switch quoted, and one reached as a dependency or by a bare
.Cm upgrade
is kept at the version it has, with a line saying so.
A repository going backwards is either somebody rolling a package back on
purpose or a repository that has been rolled back for them, and only the first
of those types the package name.
.It Fl -allow-stale
Accept a repository index whose serial is behind one this machine has already
accepted, or whose expiry has passed.
Both are refused by default; see
.Sx REPOSITORY FRESHNESS .
The machine that most needs an update is sometimes the one that has been
switched off for a year, so the refusal is overridable \(em conspicuously, on
the command line, and never implied by
.Fl y .
.It Fl -root Ar dir
Operate on another root filesystem: an image being assembled, or a disk mounted
somewhere.
The database, the cache and the configuration are all taken from under that
directory.
Hooks act on the running system, so they are not run for another root and
.Nm
says so rather than running them against the wrong machine.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/cache/sowa/staging" -compact
.It Pa /etc/sowa/pkg.conf
where the repository is;
.Xr sowa-pkg.conf 5 .
.It Pa /etc/sowa/keys/sowa-repo.pub
the Ed25519 key the index must be signed with.
.It Pa /var/lib/sowa/db
the local database: one directory per installed package.
.It Pa /var/lib/sowa/repo/index
the verified index, and
.Pa index.sig
beside it.
.It Pa /var/lib/sowa/repo/serial
the highest index serial this machine has ever accepted.
It is written only after an index has been accepted whole, and only ever
upwards.
.It Pa /var/lib/sowa/lock
held for the length of a transaction, so two runs cannot overlap.
.It Pa /var/cache/sowa/pkg
downloaded archives.
.It Pa /var/cache/sowa/staging
where an archive is unpacked before its files are moved into place.
.It Pa /var/log/sowa-pkg.log
what was asked for, what the plan was, which package went in or came out, which
declared step ran, and how it ended.
Only the commands that change something write to it, and a write that fails is
allowed to fail: a read-only command still works for somebody who cannot write
to
.Pa /var/log .
With
.Fl -root
the record is written inside that root, because it belongs with the system that
changed.
It is rotated by
.Xr logrotate 8
under
.Pa /etc/logrotate.d/sowa .
.El
.Sh VERSIONS AND BUILD IDENTITY
A package version is its upstream version followed by Sowa's numeric release
counter.
It is the update contract:
.Nm
plans an upgrade only when the repository version is newer.
A patch, recipe change, dependency rebuild or unchanged-source rebuild meant
for installed machines therefore requires a release-counter bump.
.Pp
Every package separately carries a
.Va pkgbuild
which the build derives from the manifest and installed metadata
together with the recipe/input key, compiler/toolchain identity, and dependency
build keys.
It therefore identifies the audited build rather than only bytes that happen to
be identical.
.Pp
The build id appears in the immutable archive filename, package metadata,
signed index, installed database and audit log.
It keeps builds unambiguous and lets
.Nm
check that an archive carries the identity promised by the index, but it is not
used for update ordering.
Two clean build hosts may therefore publish different build ids and archive
SHA-256 values at the same package version without replacing packages already
installed.
With an equal version,
.Fl -reinstall
is the explicit way to replace one.
.Sh REPOSITORY FRESHNESS
A signature proves who wrote an index.
It proves nothing about whether that index is the current one, and anybody who
can answer for the repository URL \(em a network in between, a caching proxy, a
mirror, or the server itself once it is somebody else's \(em can hand back last
year's index and last year's archives indefinitely without forging anything.
Every signature verifies, every checksum matches, and the machine is simply held
at a version whose defects are public while
.Ic sowa-pkg upgrade -y
reports that there is nothing to do.
.Pp
So the index carries two numbers of its own, inside the bytes the signature
covers:
.Bl -tag -width serial -compact
.It Va serial
when it was published, as seconds since the epoch.
.It Va expires
after which it is not to be believed at all.
.El
.Pp
.Nm
keeps the highest serial it has ever accepted in
.Pa /var/lib/sowa/repo/serial
and refuses a lower one.
It refuses an expired one.
And once it has seen a serial, it refuses an index that no longer carries one,
because that is the same replay with the header taken off.
A repository that has never published a serial is still readable, with a warning
saying that an old index cannot be told from the current one.
.Pp
.Fl -allow-stale
takes any of those anyway and says so on the terminal.
.Pp
The expiry is the reason a repository has to be republished on a schedule rather
than only when a package changes: if it runs out, every machine reading that
repository stops updating until it is signed again.
.Sh WHAT IS CHECKED BEFORE ANYTHING IS WRITTEN
The index is a document that drives filesystem operations, so a valid signature
over a malformed one is not enough.
Before an index replaces the one in use, every entry must have a usable package
name, version, architecture, archive basename, 64-hex SHA-256 and positive
bounded size; no package or archive name may appear twice; every dependency must
be a name the same index offers; the dependency graph must be acyclic; and no
line may carry a control character.
.Pp
Before a package is written into the root, every path in its manifest must be
relative and normalised \(em no leading slash, no
.Pa ..
component, no control character \(em and no path may sit below a path the same
manifest calls a file or a link.
Under
.Fl -root
the directory each entry will be written into is then resolved and required to
land below that root, which is what a string
.Pa ${ROOT}/${path}
cannot see: a
.Pa usr/lib
that is a link to
.Pa /
in an image being repaired.
The manifests already in a root's database are checked the same way before a
removal, because on another root somebody else wrote them.
.Pp
This is name resolution rather than
.Xr openat2 2
with
.Dv RESOLVE_BENEATH ,
which a shell cannot ask for.
It closes a tree that already contains the link.
It is not a defence against something rewriting the tree while
.Nm
is running \(em though the lock keeps a second
.Nm
out.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 0 -compact
.It 0
done.
.It 1
nothing was changed, and why is on standard error.
.It 2
the packages are installed, but a step one of them declared did not run.
.El
.Pp
The third is its own status on purpose: files in place and a setup step that
failed is neither a success nor a failed transaction, and answering 0 would let
.Ic sowa-pkg install foo && use-foo
go on to use something that was never finished being set up.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Fetch the index, then see what is out of date:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sowa-pkg update
sowa-pkg list --upgradable
.Ed
.Pp
Install something, without being asked:
.Pp
.Dl sowa-pkg install -y nginx
.Pp
Find out what a file belongs to, and what else that package owns:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sowa-pkg owns /usr/bin/dig
sowa-pkg files bind | less
.Ed
.Pp
See what a package would do to the machine before installing it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sowa-pkg info guix
sowa-pkg hooks guix
.Ed
.Pp
Check the system against its own manifests after a crash or a disk error:
.Pp
.Dl sowa-pkg verify
.Pp
Install into a disk mounted elsewhere, from a rescue system or after
.Xr sowa-bootstrap 8 :
.Pp
.Dl sowa-pkg --root /mnt install haproxy
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sowa-pkg.conf 5 ,
.Xr chkconfig 8 ,
.Xr service 8 ,
.Xr sowa-bootstrap 8 ,
.Xr sowa-chroot 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Changing the running system needs root.
Another root needs only write access to it, which is what makes
.Fl -root
usable without privilege.
.Pp
Some packages cannot be removed:
.Sy sowa-base ,
.Sy bash ,
.Sy linux ,
.Sy sowa-init ,
.Sy sowa-release ,
.Sy tar
and
.Sy xz .
Removing any of them would leave a system unable to boot, run a shell, or
install anything ever again - the last two are on the list because every package
is an XZ-compressed tar archive, so a system without them could not install
them back.
.Pp
There is no dependency solver worth the name here: dependencies are resolved
depth first from the index and installed in order.
Nothing tracks which packages were installed only as a dependency, so nothing
removes them again.
